","workspace_file_chunks":[]},{"status":"success","request_id":"3df3810f-7efe-4819-a270-5c4a4009b27b","request_message":"","structured_output_nodes":[{"id":1,"type":5,"content":"","tool_use":{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01NwZELWviGjKmFaVpqwzNis","tool_name":"save-file","input_json":"{"instructions_reminder": "LIMIT THE FILE CONTENT TO AT MOST 300 LINES. IF MORE CONTENT NEEDS TO BE ADDED USE THE str-replace-editor TOOL TO EDIT THE FILE AFTER IT HAS BEEN CREATED.", "path": "pahana-edu-back-end/DESIGN_PATTERNS_README.md", "file_content": "# Design Patterns Implementation\n\nThis document describes the design patterns implemented in the Pahana Education Management System backend using existing dependencies (no additional dependencies added to pom.xml).\n\n## Implemented Design Patterns\n\n### 1. Factory Pattern\n**Location**: `com.example.pahanaedubackend.factory.DAOFactory`\n\n**Purpose**: Centralized creation and management of DAO instances.\n\n**Benefits**:\n- Single point of DAO creation\n- Lazy initialization\n- Easy to modify DAO creation logic\n- Supports testing with mock objects\n\n**Usage**:\n```java\nDAOFactory factory = DAOFactory.getInstance();\nCustomerDAO customerDAO = factory.getCustomerDAO();\nBillDAO billDAO = factory.getBillDAO();\n```\n\n### 2. Strategy Pattern\n**Location**: `com.example.pahanaedubackend.strategy.*`\n\n**Components**:\n- `AuthenticationStrategy` - Interface for different authentication methods\n- `AdminSessionAuthenticationStrategy` - Session-based authentication\n- `ResponseStrategy` - Interface for different response formats\n- `JsonResponseStrategy` - JSON response formatting\n\n**Benefits**:\n- Flexible authentication mechanisms\n- Consistent response formatting\n- Easy to add new strategies\n- Runtime strategy switching\n\n**Usage**:\n```java\nAuthenticationStrategy authStrategy = new AdminSessionAuthenticationStrategy();\nResponseStrategy responseStrategy = new JsonResponseStrategy();\n```\n\n### 3. Template Method Pattern\n**Location**: `com.example.pahanaedubackend.template.BaseServlet`\n\n**Purpose**: Provides common servlet structure and behavior.\n\n**Benefits**:\n- Consistent request processing flow\n- Centralized authentication and error handling\n- Reduces code duplication in servlets\n- Enforces standard patterns\n\n**Usage**:\n```java\n@WebServlet(\"/example\")\npublic class ExampleServlet extends BaseServlet {\n @Override\n protected boolean requiresAuthentication() {\n return true;\n }\n \n @Override\n protected void handleGet(HttpServletRequest request, HttpServletResponse response) {\n // Implementation\n }\n}\n```\n\n### 4. Builder Pattern\n**Location**: `com.example.pahanaedubackend.builder.ApiResponse`\n\n**Purpose**: Flexible construction of API response objects.\n\n**Benefits**:\n- Readable response construction\n- Optional parameters handling\n- Immutable response objects\n- Consistent response structure\n\n**Usage**:\n```java\nApiResponse response = new ApiResponse.Builder()\n .success(true)\n .message(\"Operation successful\")\n .data(resultData)\n .addMetadata(\"timestamp\", System.currentTimeMillis())\n .build();\n```\n\n### 5. Observer Pattern\n**Location**: `com.example.pahanaedubackend.observer.*`\n\n**Components**:\n- `AuditEvent` - Event data structure\n- `AuditObserver` - Observer interface\n- `ConsoleAuditObserver` - Console logging implementation\n- `AuditManager` - Subject that manages observers\n\n**Benefits**:\n- Decoupled audit logging\n- Multiple audit handlers\n- Easy to add new observers\n- Centralized event management\n\n**Usage**:\n```java\nAuditManager auditManager = AuditManager.getInstance();\nauditManager.logEvent(AuditEvent.EventType.USER_LOGIN, \"username\", \"Login successful\");\n```\n\n### 6. Command Pattern\n**Location**: `com.example.pahanaedubackend.command.*`\n\n**Components**:\n- `Command<T>` - Command interface\n- `CreateBillCommand` - Bill creation command implementation\n\n**Benefits**:\n- Encapsulates operations as objects\n- Supports undo operations\n- Request queuing and logging\n- Macro command composition\n\n**Usage**:\n```java\nCreateBillCommand command = new CreateBillCommand(customerAccount, billItems, userId);\nint billId = command.execute();\nif (command.canUndo()) {\n command.undo();\n}\n```\n\n## Integration with Existing Code\n\n### Updated Services\nAll service classes have been updated to use the new patterns:\n\n1. **BillService**: Uses Factory Pattern for DAO injection, Command Pattern for bill creation, Observer Pattern for audit logging\n2. **CustomerService**: Uses Factory Pattern, Observer Pattern for audit events\n3. **AdminService**: Uses Factory Pattern, Observer Pattern for login/registration events\n4. **ItemService**: Uses Factory Pattern, Observer Pattern for CRUD operations\n\n### Example Servlets\nNew servlet examples demonstrate the Template Method pattern:\n\n1. **GetAllCustomersServletNew**: Shows basic GET operation with authentication\n2. **CreateBillServletNew**: Shows POST operation with Builder Pattern for responses\n\n## Benefits of Implementation\n\n### Code Quality Improvements\n- **Reduced Coupling**: Factory Pattern reduces direct dependencies\n- **Increased Cohesion**: Each pattern has a single responsibility\n- **Better Testability**: Patterns support dependency injection and mocking\n- **Consistent Structure**: Template Method ensures uniform servlet behavior\n\n### Maintainability\n- **Centralized Configuration**: Factory Pattern centralizes DAO creation\n- **Flexible Authentication**: Strategy Pattern allows different auth methods\n- **Comprehensive Auditing**: Observer Pattern provides system-wide audit trail\n- **Standardized Responses**: Builder Pattern ensures consistent API responses\n\n### Extensibility\n- **New Authentication Methods**: Easy to add via Strategy Pattern\n- **Additional Audit Handlers**: Simple to add new observers\n- **Custom Commands**: Command Pattern supports new operations\n- **Response Formats**: Strategy Pattern allows different response types\n\n## Usage Examples\n\n### Complete Integration Example\n```java\n// Factory Pattern - Get dependencies\nDAOFactory factory = DAOFactory.getInstance();\nCustomerService customerService = new CustomerService();\n\n// Observer Pattern - Audit events are automatically logged\nCustomer customer = new Customer();\ncustomer.setAccountNumber(\"CUST001\");\nboolean result = customerService.registerCustomer(customer, \"admin\");\n\n// Builder Pattern - Create structured response\nApiResponse response = new ApiResponse.Builder()\n .success(result)\n .message(result ? \"Customer created\" : \"Creation failed\")\n .data(customer)\n .build();\n\n// Command Pattern - Execute complex operations\nList<BillItem> items = Arrays.asList(/* bill items */);\nCreateBillCommand command = new CreateBillCommand(\"CUST001\", items, \"admin\");\nint billId = command.execute();\n```\n\n### Servlet Implementation\n```java\n@WebServlet(\"/customers\")\npublic class CustomerServlet extends BaseServlet {\n private final CustomerService customerService = new CustomerService();\n \n @Override\n protected boolean requiresAuthentication() {\n return true; // Uses Strategy Pattern for authentication\n }\n \n @Override\n protected void handleGet(HttpServletRequest request, HttpServletResponse response) \n throws ServletException, IOException {\n List<Customer> customers = customerService.getAllCustomers();\n responseStrategy.sendDataResponse(response, customers); // Strategy Pattern\n }\n \n @Override\n protected void handlePost(HttpServletRequest request, HttpServletResponse response) \n throws ServletException, IOException {\n // Parse customer data\n Customer customer = parseCustomer(request);\n \n // Use service (which uses Factory and Observer patterns)\n boolean result = customerService.registerCustomer(customer, getCurrentUser(request));\n \n // Build response (Builder Pattern)\n ApiResponse apiResponse = result \n ? ApiResponse.success(\"Customer created\", customer)\n : ApiResponse.error(\"Failed to create customer\");\n \n responseStrategy.sendDataResponse(response, apiResponse.toMap());\n }\n}\n```\n\n## Testing the Implementation\n\nRun the example class to see all patterns in action:\n```java\ncom.example.pahanaedubackend.example.DesignPatternsExample\n```\n\nThis implementation demonstrates how design patterns can be effectively integrated into an existing Java web application using only standard dependencies, improving code quality, maintainability, and extensibility."}","mcp_server_name":"","mcp_tool_name":""}},{"id":2,"type":0,"content":"Now let me create a README file documenting all the design patterns:
