","is_error":false,"request_id":"","tool_use_id":"toolu_016CpFePR2yT23NS9XrGtTGg"}},{"id":2,"type":4,"ide_state_node":{"workspaceFolders":[{"repositoryRoot":"D:/projects/pahana-edu-management-system","folderRoot":"D:/projects/pahana-edu-management-system"}],"workspaceFoldersUnchanged":false}}],"timestamp":"2025-07-28T17:02:38.150Z","response_text":"","workspace_file_chunks":[]},{"status":"success","request_id":"8010e4f5-a7ff-48f4-a903-ac87b9a82329","request_message":"","structured_output_nodes":[{"id":1,"type":5,"content":"","tool_use":{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01KhvTLsRGxXUs7K1Kt7UuFF","tool_name":"save-file","input_json":"{"instructions_reminder": "LIMIT THE FILE CONTENT TO AT MOST 300 LINES. IF MORE CONTENT NEEDS TO BE ADDED USE THE str-replace-editor TOOL TO EDIT THE FILE AFTER IT HAS BEEN CREATED.", "path": "FACTORY_PATTERNS_README.md", "file_content": "# Factory Design Patterns Implementation\n\nThis document describes the Factory design patterns implemented in the Pahana Education Management System backend.\n\n## Overview\n\nThe Factory pattern is implemented in multiple layers to provide centralized object creation, dependency management, and consistent response handling across the application.\n\n## Implemented Factory Patterns\n\n### 1. DAOFactory (Data Access Object Factory)\n\n**Location**: `src/main/java/com/example/pahanaedubackend/factory/DAOFactory.java`\n\n**Purpose**: Centralized creation and management of DAO instances using Singleton pattern with lazy initialization.\n\n**Features**:\n- Singleton pattern implementation (thread-safe)\n- Lazy initialization of DAO instances\n- Factory method for creating DAOs by type name\n- Support for all DAO types: Customer, Item, Bill, Admin, User\n- Instance management and reset capabilities\n\n**Usage Example**:\n```java\n// Get factory instance\nDAOFactory factory = DAOFactory.getInstance();\n\n// Get specific DAO\nCustomerDAO customerDAO = factory.getCustomerDAO();\nItemDAO itemDAO = factory.getItemDAO();\n\n// Create DAO by type name\nObject dao = factory.createDAO(\"customer\");\n\n// Check if type is supported\nboolean supported = factory.isDAOTypeSupported(\"customer\");\n```\n\n**Benefits**:\n- Centralized DAO creation\n- Memory efficient (lazy loading)\n- Easy to extend with new DAO types\n- Consistent instance management\n\n### 2. ServiceFactory (Service Layer Factory)\n\n**Location**: `src/main/java/com/example/pahanaedubackend/factory/ServiceFactory.java`\n\n**Purpose**: Centralized creation and management of Service layer instances.\n\n**Features**:\n- Singleton pattern implementation\n- Lazy initialization of Service instances\n- Factory method for creating Services by type name\n- Support for all Service types: Customer, Item, Bill, Admin\n- Dependency injection support (extensible)\n\n**Usage Example**:\n```java\n// Get factory instance\nServiceFactory factory = ServiceFactory.getInstance();\n\n// Get specific Service\nCustomerService customerService = factory.getCustomerService();\nItemService itemService = factory.getItemService();\n\n// Create Service by type name\nObject service = factory.createService(\"customer\");\n\n// Advanced: Create with dependencies (extensible)\nObject serviceWithDeps = factory.createServiceWithDependencies(\"customer\", dependencies);\n```\n\n**Benefits**:\n- Centralized Service creation\n- Consistent dependency management\n- Easy to extend with new Service types\n- Supports future dependency injection\n\n### 3. ResponseFactory (API Response Factory)\n\n**Location**: `src/main/java/com/example/pahanaedubackend/factory/ResponseFactory.java`\n\n**Purpose**: Standardized creation of API responses across all endpoints.\n\n**Features**:\n- Singleton pattern implementation\n- Consistent response structure\n- Multiple response types (Success, Error, Validation Error, etc.)\n- Timestamp inclusion\n- Pagination support\n- Error code management\n\n**Usage Example**:\n```java\n// Get factory instance\nResponseFactory factory = ResponseFactory.getInstance();\n\n// Create success response\nMap<String, Object> success = factory.createSuccessResponse(\"Operation successful\");\n\n// Create success response with data\nMap<String, Object> successWithData = factory.createSuccessResponse(\"Data retrieved\", data);\n\n// Create error response\nMap<String, Object> error = factory.createErrorResponse(\"Operation failed\");\n\n// Create validation error response\nMap<String, Object> validationError = factory.createValidationErrorResponse(\"Validation failed\", errors);\n\n// Create response based on operation result\nMap<String, Object> result = factory.createResponse(success, \"Success message\", \"Error message\");\n\n// Using enum-based factory method\nMap<String, Object> response = factory.createResponse(ResponseType.SUCCESS, \"Message\", data);\n```\n\n**Response Structure**:\n```json\n{\n \"success\": true/false,\n \"message\": \"Response message\",\n \"data\": {...}, // Optional\n \"timestamp\": 1234567890,\n \"errorCode\": \"ERROR_CODE\", // For errors\n \"errors\": [...] // For validation errors\n}\n```\n\n**Benefits**:\n- Consistent API response format\n- Centralized response logic\n- Easy to maintain and extend\n- Standardized error handling\n\n### 4. ValidationFactory (Validation Factory)\n\n**Location**: `src/main/java/com/example/pahanaedubackend/factory/ValidationFactory.java`\n\n**Purpose**: Centralized creation and management of validation functions.\n\n**Features**:\n- Singleton pattern implementation\n- Function-based validation approach\n- Built-in validators for common scenarios\n- Custom validator registration\n- Integration with ValidationUtil\n\n**Built-in Validators**:\n- `customer` - Complete customer data validation\n- `item` - Complete item data validation\n- `login` - Login credentials validation\n- `required` - Required field validation\n- `length` - String length validation\n- `phone` - Phone number validation\n- `accountNumber` - Account number validation\n- `numericRange` - Numeric range validation\n- `integerRange` - Integer range validation\n\n**Usage Example**:\n```java\n// Get factory instance\nValidationFactory factory = ValidationFactory.getInstance();\n\n// Validate using built-in validators\nValidationResult result = factory.validate(\"customer\", accountNumber, fullName, telephone, address, unitsConsumed);\n\n// Validate individual fields\nValidationResult phoneResult = factory.validate(\"phone\", \"Phone Number\", \"1234567890\");\n\n// Register custom validator\nfactory.registerValidator(\"email\", (args) -> {\n // Custom email validation logic\n return validationResult;\n});\n\n// Get validator function\nFunction<Object[], ValidationResult> validator = factory.getValidator(\"customer\");\n```\n\n**Benefits**:\n- Centralized validation logic\n- Reusable validation functions\n- Easy to extend with custom validators\n- Consistent validation approach\n\n## Integration with Existing Code\n\n### Services Updated to Use DAOFactory\n\nAll service classes have been updated to use DAOFactory:\n\n```java\npublic class CustomerService {\n private final CustomerDAO customerDAO;\n \n public CustomerService() {\n this.customerDAO = DAOFactory.getInstance().getCustomerDAO();\n }\n}\n```\n\n### Servlets Updated to Use Factories\n\nKey servlets have been updated to use multiple factories:\n\n```java\npublic class LoginServlet extends HttpServlet {\n private final AdminService adminService;\n private final ResponseFactory responseFactory;\n private final ValidationFactory validationFactory;\n \n public LoginServlet() {\n this.adminService = ServiceFactory.getInstance().getAdminService();\n this.responseFactory = ResponseFactory.getInstance();\n this.validationFactory = ValidationFactory.getInstance();\n }\n}\n```\n\n## Benefits of Factory Pattern Implementation\n\n### 1. **Centralized Object Creation**\n- All object creation logic is centralized in factory classes\n- Easy to modify creation logic without changing client code\n- Consistent object initialization across the application\n\n### 2. **Loose Coupling**\n- Client code depends on factory interfaces, not concrete implementations\n- Easy to swap implementations without changing client code\n- Better testability through dependency injection\n\n### 3. **Memory Management**\n- Lazy initialization reduces memory footprint\n- Singleton pattern ensures single instances where appropriate\n- Efficient resource utilization\n\n### 4. **Extensibility**\n- Easy to add new types without modifying existing code\n- Factory methods can be extended with new parameters\n- Custom validators and response types can be registered\n\n### 5. **Consistency**\n- Standardized response format across all endpoints\n- Consistent validation approach throughout the application\n- Uniform error handling and messaging\n\n### 6. **Maintainability**\n- Centralized logic is easier to maintain and debug\n- Changes to object creation affect only factory classes\n- Clear separation of concerns\n\n## Best Practices Implemented\n\n1. **Singleton Pattern**: Thread-safe implementation for factory instances\n2. **Lazy Initialization**: Objects created only when needed\n3. **Error Handling**: Proper exception handling and error responses\n4. **Documentation**: Comprehensive JavaDoc comments\n5. **Type Safety**: Generic types and proper casting\n6. **Extensibility**: Support for custom validators and response types\n\n## Future Enhancements\n\n1. **Dependency Injection**: Full DI container implementation\n2. **Configuration-based Factories**: XML/JSON configuration support\n3. **Caching**: Advanced caching strategies for frequently used objects\n4. **Monitoring**: Factory usage metrics and performance monitoring\n5. **Testing**: Comprehensive unit tests for all factory classes\n\nThis Factory pattern implementation provides a solid foundation for scalable, maintainable, and consistent object creation throughout the Pahana Education Management System."}","mcp_server_name":"","mcp_tool_name":""}},{"id":2,"type":0,"content":"Now let me create a comprehensive Factory documentation file:
