Path: pahana-edu-back-end/DESIGN_PATTERNS_README.md
 1	# Design Patterns Implementation
 2	
 3	This document describes the design patterns implemented in the Pahana Education Management System backend using existing dependencies (no additional dependencies added to pom.xml).
 4	
 5	## Implemented Design Patterns
 6	
 7	### 1. Factory Pattern
 8	**Location**: `com.example.pahanaedubackend.factory.DAOFactory`
 9	
 10	**Purpose**: Centralized creation and management of DAO instances.
 11	
 12	**Benefits**:
 13	- Single point of DAO creation
 14	- Lazy initialization
 15	- Easy to modify DAO creation logic
 16	- Supports testing with mock objects
 17	
 18	**Usage**:
 19	```java
 20	DAOFactory factory = DAOFactory.getInstance();
 21	CustomerDAO customerDAO = factory.getCustomerDAO();
 22	BillDAO billDAO = factory.getBillDAO();
 23	```
...
 45	
 46	### 3. Template Method Pattern
 47	**Location**: `com.example.pahanaedubackend.template.BaseServlet`
 48	
 49	**Purpose**: Provides common servlet structure and behavior.
 50	
 51	**Benefits**:
 52	- Consistent request processing flow
 53	- Centralized authentication and error handling
 54	- Reduces code duplication in servlets
 55	- Enforces standard patterns
 56	
 57	**Usage**:
 58	```java
 59	@WebServlet("/example")
 60	public class ExampleServlet extends BaseServlet {
 61	 @Override
 62	 protected boolean requiresAuthentication() {
 63	 return true;
 64	 }
 65	 
 66	 @Override
 67	 protected void handleGet(HttpServletRequest request, HttpServletResponse response) {
 68	 // Implementation
 69	 }
 70	}
...
 135	```
 136	
 137	## Integration with Existing Code
 138	
 139	### Updated Services
 140	All service classes have been updated to use the new patterns:
 141	
 142	1. **BillService**: Uses Factory Pattern for DAO injection, Command Pattern for bill creation, Observer Pattern for audit logging
 143	2. **CustomerService**: Uses Factory Pattern, Observer Pattern for audit events
 144	3. **AdminService**: Uses Factory Pattern, Observer Pattern for login/registration events
 145	4. **ItemService**: Uses Factory Pattern, Observer Pattern for CRUD operations
 146	
 147	### Example Servlets
 148	New servlet examples demonstrate the Template Method pattern:
 149	
 150	1. **GetAllCustomersServletNew**: Shows basic GET operation with authentication
 151	2. **CreateBillServletNew**: Shows POST operation with Builder Pattern for responses
...
 198	
 199	### Servlet Implementation
 200	```java
 201	@WebServlet("/customers")
 202	public class CustomerServlet extends BaseServlet {
 203	 private final CustomerService customerService = new CustomerService();
 204	 
 205	 @Override
 206	 protected boolean requiresAuthentication() {
 207	 return true; // Uses Strategy Pattern for authentication
 208	 }
 209	 
 210	 @Override
 211	 protected void handleGet(HttpServletRequest request, HttpServletResponse response) 
 212	 throws ServletException, IOException {
 213	 List<Customer> customers = customerService.getAllCustomers();
 214	 responseStrategy.sendDataResponse(response, customers); // Strategy Pattern
 215	 }
